1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:01,894
The final section.

2
00:00:02,174 --> 00:00:05,140
Let's look back at what we built, and where you can go next.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,520
This is your project.

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:17,954
Sources and seeds, staging, an intermediate model with tested business rules, marts, a snapshot keeping history, and an exposure for a real analysis.

5
00:00:18,234 --> 00:00:21,072
Real data, real problems, real decisions.

6
00:00:22,172 --> 00:00:23,921
Remember the map from section two?

7
00:00:24,201 --> 00:00:31,646
Every planned block is ticked: models, sources, seeds, tests, macros, analyses, snapshots, docs and exposures.

8
00:00:31,926 --> 00:00:36,022
The dashed ones, like metrics and the semantic layer, are your next steps.

9
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:41,197
If you keep only three habits: give the agent context, not memory.

10
00:00:41,477 --> 00:00:43,440
Read every diff before you keep it.

11
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:45,490
And let dbt be the judge.

12
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:49,909
The agent made us faster, but every decision in this project was ours.

13
00:00:51,009 --> 00:00:59,265
The dbt commands we used, all from version one: debug, seed, run and build, test, compile, deps, snapshot, and docs.

14
00:00:59,545 --> 00:01:03,534
In Rosetta most of them are buttons, and now you know what each one does.

15
00:01:04,634 --> 00:01:05,722
Where to go next.

16
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:09,416
The semantic layer, to define metrics like revenue once.

17
00:01:09,696 --> 00:01:12,981
Contracts and groups, when other teams depend on your models.

18
00:01:13,261 --> 00:01:15,864
Python models, which DuckDB supports.

19
00:01:16,144 --> 00:01:19,600
And automatic builds on every change, with Rosetta Cloud.
